  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{4}=0.\n$$",
